<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Mandatory Iraq</h1><p class="st-meta">1920 CE – 1932 CE · capital Baghdad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British-run League of Nations mandate that created the modern state of Iraq from three Ottoman provinces and installed a Hashemite king.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>British and Indian troops landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> in November 1914, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1917 and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> of 1918. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-remo-conference">San Remo conference</a> of April 1920 assigned the former Ottoman provinces of Basra, Baghdad and Mosul to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a>. The great revolt of 1920 against British rule was suppressed at heavy cost.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo-conference">Cairo Conference</a> of 1921, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> chose the Hashemite prince Faisal, recently expelled from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>, as king; after a staged referendum he was crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in August 1921. British High Commissioners such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percy-cox">Sir Percy Cox</a> advised the monarchy, while the Anglo-Iraqi treaties kept British control of foreign policy, finance and air bases. The League awarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> region to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 1925, confirmed by treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> in 1926, and oil was struck at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baba-gurgur">Baba Gurgur</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirkuk">Kirkuk</a> in 1927.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-iraqi-treaty-1930">Anglo-Iraqi Treaty</a> of 1930, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> as an independent state in October 1932, ending the mandate; the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-iraq">Kingdom of Iraq</a> continued with strong British ties.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mandatory-iraq.jpg" alt="Mandatory Iraq" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday (talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Arab_Federation.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1920 CE – 1932 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Baghdad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Percy Cox, Faisal I, Francis Humphrys</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia and Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Kurdish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iraq</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Mandate of Mesopotamia, Mandate of Iraq</dd></dl></aside></div>
